  {
    "event": {
      "address": "136 Shaftesbury Avenue, London W1D 5EZ",
      "all_day": false,
      "category": "music",
      "dedupe_key": "from-one-glasse-to-another-day-1|2026-12-11|stone-nest",
      "description": "Doors 7pm, Performance 7.30pm The Leap is a one-woman performance by vocalist Nora Fischer, except for the unexpected twist that Singer Nora will not be present on stage. Instead, Person Nora will be explaining why she will not be singing. For a while, she was unable to do so because of a complex cramp that was keeping her larynx in such a strong headlock that even Happy Birthday for her best friends was no longer an option. How could such a thing happen? With this burning question, Nora starts digging for answers: from the pressure of the world's largest stages, to the inner pressure that she had to remove from her system like a wasp's poisonous sting. She unravels how the joy with which she once produced her frst notes, gradually got overshadowed by piercing eyes and razor sharp judgments. There is no shortage of demons behind the successful performer she had become – and among those demons is Nora's sister, who falls deeper and deeper into darkness as Nora's voice falls into silence. But Nora is not the type to simply give up: there is a reason her parents used to call her “The Tank”. In the midst of the ruins, she demands answers: how can the radiating joy with which two girls started their lives, evolve into such an all-encompassing cramp? Who told us to get so caught up in suffocating perfectionism? In this grippingly tragic yet humorous storytelling performance, Nora invites her audience to join her in a highly intimate disclosure of what we secretly all know: nothing is what it seems. ‍ The Leap is presented by Musarc as part of a transdisciplinary festival of voice and video. …from one glasse to another explores the myth of Echo and Narcissus in relationship to voice, performance and technology, where recent translations and reformulations, reveal nuanced and radical approaches to both gender and audio-visual phenomena. Curated by Sam Belinfante.",
      "ends_at": null,
      "image_url": "https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/61f2dd13b69b2408c0d49b6b/6acf60f50ba266b5a39212b8_Leap-landscape%20empty.avif",
      "lat": null,
      "lng": null,
      "price": {
        "currency": null,
        "is_free": false,
        "max": null,
        "min": null
      },
      "starts_at": "2026-12-11T19:30:00Z",
      "tags": [],
      "title": "…from one glasse to another. Day 1.",
      "url": "https://www.stonenest.org/events/from-one-glasse-to-another-day-1",
      "venue_name": "Stone Nest"
    },
    "source_event_id": "from-one-glasse-to-another-day-1"
  },
  {
    "event": {
      "address": "136 Shaftesbury Avenue, London W1D 5EZ",
      "all_day": false,
      "category": "music",
      "dedupe_key": "from-one-glasse-to-another-day-2|2026-12-12|stone-nest",
      "description": "Doors 5pm, performance at 5.30pm Doors 7.30pm, performance 8pm Musarc performs work by Philip Glass, Lina Lapelyte, Meredith Monk, Pauline Oliveros, Sarah Kate Wilson, Laure Prouvost, Ben Rivers and Ralph Vaughan Williams. Day 2 of …from one glasse to another, a transdisciplinary festival of voice and video curated by Sam Belinfante. Featuring work by Lina Lapelyte, Laure Prouvost, Ben Rivers, Ralph Vaughan Williams and Philip Glass, performed by Musarc with Patricia Auchterlonie and Plus-Minus Ensemble. Musical Director: Oli Kitching. In Ovid’s story Echo and Narcissus play creatively and provocatively with sonic and visual image making; Echo – particularly – is able to conjure new meaning and form through vocal repetition and mimicry. Beyond profound relations between light and sound, the stories are replete with issues of emptiness and fullness of self, of absorption and reflection. Musarc presents a series of musical vignettes that restage Echo and Narcissus’ dialogues through modern and seemingly outmoded audiovisual technology. Further information on the programme: ‍ In his early, rarely performed partsong; Echo's Lament for Narcissus (1895–96), Ralph Vaughan Williams teases out the temporal and synaesthetic distortions of Ben Jonson’s poem of the same name. This extraordinary setting forms the basis of a series of new reflections and voicings of Echo and Narcissus that reveal nuanced and radical approaches to both gender and audio-visual phenomena. Recalling her recent performances and video installations at The Cosmic House (2025), artist and composer Lina Lapelyte presents a series of live and filmed fragments where performers ponder, commune, and weave new interpretations of cosmic themes. Further attuning to the world Musarc also present Pauline Oliveros’s radical Tuning Meditation (1971), this time paired with the artist Sarah Kate Wilson’s Rainbows, first presented by the artist and Musarc at Palais de Tokyo, Paris in 2017. In Laure Prouvost’s film Shadow Does (2023) we see the world through the eyes of a little girl, who in shadow play, explpres the objects of her daily life and rapidly changing habits. The film is further layered with songs by Sam Belinfante and interventions by the extraordinary singer and maker Patricia Auchterlonie. Ben Rivers takes us deeper into post-apocalyptic earth with his film Golden Cities, an extract from his award winning Mare’s Nest, based on Don DeLillo's climate crisis play The Word for Snow (2014). In counterpoint, Meredith Monk’s Earth Seen from Above – from her 1991 opera ATLAS – reflects an earth traversed as both spiritual odyssey and embodied choreography. The culmination of the event is a multimedia performance of ‘Vessels’, from Philip Glass’ seminal score for Godfrey Reggio’s Koyaanisqatsi (1982). Even after the excessive proliferation of timelapse imagery, now a signifier of stock video and AI slop, the music and visuals of Koyaanisqatsi remain staggeringly prophetic in the re-imagining of people, technology and our environment. \"It's not that we use technology, we live technology. Technology has become as ubiquitous as the air we breathe...\" - Godfrey Reggio, 2022 Image Credits Lead Image: Lina Lapelytė, In the Dark, We Play, 2025, video still by Martynas Norvaišas, commissioned by the Jencks Foundation at The Cosmic House. Second Image: Musarc Winter Konsert 2025. Photo: Yiannis Katsaris",
      "ends_at": "2026-12-12T20:00:00Z",
      "image_url": "https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/61f2dd13b69b2408c0d49b6b/6ad0aaf37baedeb426405be5_32bc15b973bdfd7f24fef1fe1bc9962f53610982-1920x1080-2.avif",
